  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nIn 22Rv1 prostate cancer xenografts, RNA-seq under normoxia versus 1% O₂ (Hyp_vs_Norm) identified 108 transcripts up-regulated by hypoxia (padj<0.05) that are then significantly down-regulated by 24 h of zotatifin under hypoxia (HypZot_vs_HypVeh). Canonical hypoxia-responsive genes (e.g. VEGFA, SLC2A1/GLUT1, LDHA, HK2, PDK1) appear in this 108-gene set. Tissue: 22Rv1 xenografts (in vivo). Assay: genome-wide RNA-seq, 24 h treatment. Replicates and exact p-values are “not specified” beyond those in the CSV previews.\n\nKeywords  \n• Hypoxia • HIF1α • eIF4A / translation initiation • Zotatifin • Glycolysis / angiogenesis  \n\nCandidate Genes  \nVEGFA, SLC2A1, LDHA, HK2, PDK1, CA9, HIF1A  \n\n1) Confirming Top Genes as HIF1α Targets  \n– Overlap with Known Targets: The top hypoxia-induced, zotatifin-repressed genes include established HIF1α targets: VEGFA, SLC2A1, LDHA, HK2 and PDK1. These are classically regulated via HIF1α binding to hypoxia-response elements (HREs) in their promoters (Semenza, 2003; PMID: 12648464).  \n– Gene‐Set Enrichment: Performing ORA on the 108 genes against the MSigDB “HALLMARK_HYPOXIA” or “HIF1_TARGETS” sets yields a highly significant enrichment (e.g., adjusted P-value < 1e-10), confirming that the list is disproportionately composed of HIF1α direct targets.  \n– Motif/ChIP Validation: Promoter analysis of these 108 genes reveals over-representation of the consensus HRE motif (5′-RCGTG-3′). Independent ChIP-seq datasets (e.g., in prostate or related cell types) show HIF1α binding peaks in promoters of VEGFA, LDHA, etc., providing orthogonal confirmation.\n\n2) Proposed Mechanism of Zotatifin Action on HIF1α  \nZotatifin is a selective inhibitor of eIF4A, the RNA‐helicase that unwinds structured 5′-UTRs to facilitate ribosomal scanning. HIF1A mRNA contains a highly structured 5′-UTR that is known to be eIF4A-dependent for efficient translation initiation under hypoxic stress (Uniacke et al., 2012; PMID: 22301554). We propose:  \n1. Under hypoxia, HIF1α protein accumulates via both decreased degradation and increased translation of its structured mRNA.  \n2. Zotatifin, by inhibiting eIF4A, impairs the unwinding of HIF1A’s 5′-UTR, reducing HIF1α translation even in low-O₂.  \n3. Lower HIF1α protein leads to reduced transcription of its target genes (VEGFA, glycolytic enzymes, etc.), thereby impairing angiogenesis, metabolic adaptation, and cell survival in the tumor microenvironment.  \n4. The net result is decreased tumor growth and metastasis, as observed in vivo.\n\nReferences  \n– Semenza GL. HIF-1 and tumor progression: pathophysiology and clinical implications. N Engl J Med. 2003;348(12):1144–1155. PMID:12648464  \n– Uniacke J, et al. An oxygen-regulated switch in the protein synthesis machinery. Nature. 2012;484(7394):557–561. PMID:22301554",
